{
 "lead": "Real voltages are rarely pure sines. A rectifier, a triac dimmer or a phone charger chops the mains sine and leaves a periodic wave of a different shape. Fourier's theorem says any periodic wave of period T is a DC term plus sines and cosines at whole multiples of the fundamental ω = 2π/T. Each harmonic is then its own AC problem, solved with phasors at its own frequency, and the results combine by root-sum-square. Only a few universities teach this chapter; the rest stop at single-frequency AC.",
 "prereq": "You need: <a href=\"#sinusoids-rms-and-average-values-form-factor\">3.1</a> for RMS, <a href=\"#phasors-and-phasor-diagrams\">3.2</a> for phasors, <a href=\"#real-reactive-apparent-and-complex-power-power-factor\">3.5</a> for average power.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "The Fourier series of a periodic wave",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Let v(t) be periodic with period T and fundamental ω = 2π/T rad/s. Fourier's theorem writes it as a DC term plus harmonics at nω:"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>v(t) = a<sub>0</sub>/2 + Σ<sub>n=1</sub><sup>∞</sup> (a<sub>n</sub> cos nωt + b<sub>n</sub> sin nωt)</b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "a<sub>0</sub>/2 is the DC (average) value in V; a<sub>n</sub> and b<sub>n</sub> are the cosine and sine coefficients in V; n is the harmonic order; nω is the nth harmonic frequency in rad/s."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The coefficients come from one period of the wave:"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>a<sub>n</sub> = (2/T)∫<sub>0</sub><sup>T</sup> v(t) cos nωt dt,  b<sub>n</sub> = (2/T)∫<sub>0</sub><sup>T</sup> v(t) sin nωt dt</b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "T is the period in s; the integral runs over any full period. Put n = 0 in the cosine formula: a<sub>0</sub> = (2/T)∫<sub>0</sub><sup>T</sup> v(t) dt, so a<sub>0</sub>/2 = (1/T)∫<sub>0</sub><sup>T</sup> v(t) dt is the average (DC) value."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Amplitude–phase form packs each harmonic into one cosine:"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>v(t) = c<sub>0</sub> + Σ c<sub>n</sub> cos(nωt + φ<sub>n</sub>)</b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "c<sub>0</sub> = a<sub>0</sub>/2 in V; c<sub>n</sub> = √(a<sub>n</sub>² + b<sub>n</sub>²) is the peak of harmonic n in V; φ<sub>n</sub> = −tan<sup>−1</sup>(b<sub>n</sub>/a<sub>n</sub>) in rad."
    },
    {
     "type": "bullets",
     "items": [
      "Even wave, v(−t) = v(t): every b<sub>n</sub> = 0, so cosine terms only.",
      "Odd wave, v(−t) = −v(t): every a<sub>n</sub> = 0, so sine terms only.",
      "Half-wave symmetry, v(t + T/2) = −v(t): a<sub>0</sub> = 0 and all even harmonics vanish; only odd n survive."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "A square wave has half-wave symmetry, so it holds the 1st, 3rd, 5th … harmonics only, with peaks falling as 1/n."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "RMS, average power and THD",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Square v(t) and average over one period. Every cross term 2V<sub>m</sub>V<sub>n</sub> cos(mωt) cos(nωt) with m ≠ n averages to zero. So harmonics add in power, not in amplitude:"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>V<sub>rms</sub> = √(V<sub>0</sub>² + V<sub>1,rms</sub>² + V<sub>2,rms</sub>² + ⋯)</b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "V<sub>0</sub> is the DC value in V; V<sub>n,rms</sub> = c<sub>n</sub>/√2 is the RMS of harmonic n in V."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Average power follows the same rule — only voltage and current of the same frequency make a nonzero average:"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>P = V<sub>0</sub>I<sub>0</sub> + Σ<sub>n=1</sub><sup>∞</sup> V<sub>n,rms</sub> I<sub>n,rms</sub> cos φ<sub>n</sub></b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "P is in W; φ<sub>n</sub> is the phase between the nth-harmonic voltage and current in rad."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Form factor F = V<sub>rms</sub>/V<sub>avg</sub>, where V<sub>avg</sub> is the average of |v(t)| over a period. Total harmonic distortion compares the harmonics with the fundamental:"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>THD = √(V<sub>2,rms</sub>² + V<sub>3,rms</sub>² + ⋯) / V<sub>1,rms</sub></b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "THD is a pure ratio, quoted as a fraction or a percentage; the denominator is the fundamental RMS only, never the total RMS. With no DC, THD = √(V<sub>rms</sub>² − V<sub>1,rms</sub>²)/V<sub>1,rms</sub>."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Filters decide which harmonics survive: a filter that attenuates a harmonic also removes the power and the distortion that harmonic carried."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "examples": [
  {
   "question": "A voltage v(t) = 100 sin(314t) + 30 sin(942t + 30°) V is applied to a circuit. Find its RMS value and its THD.",
   "steps": [
    "Match with v(t) = V<sub>0</sub> + V<sub>1</sub> sin(ω<sub>1</sub>t + θ<sub>1</sub>) + V<sub>3</sub> sin(3ω<sub>1</sub>t + θ<sub>3</sub>). Here V<sub>0</sub> = 0, V<sub>1</sub> = 100 V, ω<sub>1</sub> = 314 rad/s, V<sub>3</sub> = 30 V, and 942 = 3 × 314, so the second term is the third harmonic.",
    "Fundamental RMS = 100/√2 = 70.71 V.",
    "Third-harmonic RMS = 30/√2 = 21.21 V.",
    "Total RMS = √(70.71² + 21.21²) = √(5000 + 450) = √5450 = 73.82 V.",
    "THD = 21.21/70.71 = 0.3000, that is 30.0 %."
   ],
   "answer": "V<sub>rms</sub> = 73.8 V and THD = 0.30 (30 %).",
   "check": "import math\nv1 = 100/math.sqrt(2)\nv3 = 30/math.sqrt(2)\nvrms = math.sqrt(v1**2 + v3**2)\nthd = v3/v1\nprint(round(v1,2))\nprint(round(v3,2))\nprint(round(vrms,2))\nprint(round(thd,4))",
   "values": [
    70.71,
    21.21,
    73.82,
    0.3
   ],
   "output": ""
  },
  {
   "question": "The voltage of the first example, v(t) = 100 sin(314t) + 30 sin(942t + 30°) V, is applied to a coil of resistance 10 Ω whose reactance is 10 Ω at 50 Hz. Find the RMS current, the power and the THD of the current.",
   "steps": [
    "Solve each harmonic as its own AC circuit. Reactance grows with frequency: X<sub>1</sub> = 10 Ω at the fundamental, X<sub>3</sub> = 3 × 10 = 30 Ω at the third harmonic.",
    "Fundamental: Z<sub>1</sub> = 10 + j10 = 14.14∠45° Ω. I<sub>1</sub> = 70.71/14.14 = 5.00 A RMS.",
    "Third harmonic: Z<sub>3</sub> = 10 + j30 = 31.62∠71.57° Ω. I<sub>3</sub> = 21.21/31.62 = 0.671 A RMS.",
    "RMS current: I = √(5.00² + 0.671²) = √(25 + 0.45) = 5.04 A.",
    "Power, harmonic by harmonic: P = 70.71 × 5.00 × cos 45° + 21.21 × 0.671 × cos 71.57° = 250.0 + 4.5 = 254.5 W. Check: I² R = 25.45 × 10 = 254.5 W.",
    "Current THD = I<sub>3</sub>/I<sub>1</sub> = 0.671/5.00 = 0.134, that is 13.4 %."
   ],
   "answer": "I = 5.04 A RMS, P = 254.5 W, current THD = 13.4 %. The voltage THD was 30 %: the coil's reactance triples at the third harmonic, so the current is closer to a sine than the voltage."
  }
 ],
 "traps": [
  "Adding harmonic RMS values directly: 70.7 + 21.2 = 91.9 V is wrong, because RMS combines as the root of the sum of squares.",
  "Expecting a third-harmonic current to produce average power with the fundamental voltage. sin(ωt)·sin(3ωt) averages to zero over a period.",
  "Leaving the DC term out of the RMS: V<sub>rms</sub>² contains V<sub>0</sub>², not harmonics alone.",
  "Putting total RMS in the THD denominator. THD is always referred to the fundamental RMS.",
  "Assuming half-wave symmetry keeps even harmonics. It removes them, and the DC, leaving odd orders only."
 ],
 "quiz": [
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "State the trigonometric Fourier series of a periodic voltage.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "v(t) = a₀/2 + Σ(aₙ cos nωt + bₙ sin nωt), n = 1 to ∞, with ω = 2π/T."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Why do harmonic RMS values combine by root-sum-square rather than by simple addition?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "The square of the sum contains cross terms 2VₘVₙ cos(mωt)cos(nωt) with m ≠ n; each averages to zero over a period, so only the squared terms survive."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "v(t) = 50 + 40 sin(ωt) + 12 sin(3ωt) V. Find V<sub>rms</sub>.",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "V<sub>rms</sub> = √(50² + 40²/2 + 12²/2) = √(2500 + 800 + 72) = √3372 = 58.07 V."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "A distorted voltage has V<sub>rms</sub> = 230 V and fundamental RMS = 225 V. Find the THD.",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "Harmonic RMS = √(230² − 225²) = √2275 = 47.70 V; THD = 47.70/225 = 0.212, that is 21.2 %."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "A wave has half-wave symmetry and no DC. Which harmonics can it contain, and why?",
   "marks": 5,
   "a": "Only odd harmonics. Shifting by T/2 negates the wave, which is exactly what odd harmonics do; even harmonics repeat after T/2 and would not be negated."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "An even periodic wave has only cosine terms. What does that say about the wave at t = 0?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "Every sine term is zero at t = 0 and every cosine term has zero slope there, so the slope of the whole wave is zero at t = 0: the wave has a turning point there and is a mirror image about t = 0. The cosines alone do not say whether that point is a maximum or a minimum; that depends on the signs of the coefficients."
  }
 ],
 "beyond": "Not for the exam. Power-quality standards limit voltage THD at the point of supply, and metering instruments compute THD from a sampled waveform rather than from coefficients. Switch-mode converters are the usual source of odd harmonics in Indian distribution feeders, and passive or active filters are chosen to remove them. Search next for total demand distortion and IEEE 519 limits if you work on industrial supply.",
 "figure": {
